      "content": "The Carbon Border Adjustment Mechanism (CBAM) aims to prevent carbon leakage by taxing imports from non-EU countries based on their embedded emissions, thereby protecting EU industries from competitive disadvantage while promoting global decarbonization. Proponents argue it creates a level playing field and drives international cooperation on climate goals, while critics contend it may act as a hidden trade barrier that risks straining diplomatic relations and imposes significant compliance costs on trading partners. Implementing CBAM requires robust data verification systems and could lead to short-term disruptions in global supply chains, yet long-term alignment with climate policies suggests aligned incentives for emissions reduction in both exported and imported goods. This balanced approach underscores a strategic shift where trade policy directly supports environmental regulations, though Estonia and other EU members already note potential benefits for its export-heavy sectors. The mechanism represents a significant regulatory evolution that may eventually expand to cover additional product categories as supply chains mature.",
